Notebook[{Cell["From the Classic SWW Model to Energy and Food Extensions", 
   "Title"], 
  Cell["Theoretical derivations for the three local implementations", 
   "Subtitle"], Cell["Expanded textbook edition, 20 October 2026. This \
chapter follows the requested order: SWW_klasyczny, SWW_energia_zywnosc, \
SWW_energia_w_produkcji. It is written in English, as in the companion \
annotated notebooks. Levels and optimality conditions are developed before \
their log-linear counterparts. The PDF is the reading edition; this Wolfram \
script and the notebook are reproducible working sources. No backups are \
created.", "Text"], Cell["Important scope distinction. This order is not a \
demonstrated development chronology. The food README describes an extension \
of an energy-model diagnostic variant. Both sectoral folders already contain \
energy in production AND household consumption. The last step in the \
requested order therefore removes imported food; it does not introduce energy \
for the first time. We compare the actual main .mod files and EA20 \
calibration, measurement and shock modules, rather than infer mechanisms from \
folder names.", "Text"], Cell["A linear model does not uniquely identify its \
nonlinear foundations. The household-habit and cost-minimisation problems \
below are explicit compatible reconstructions. The inherited SWW investment, \
wage, employment and utilisation blocks are documented with their implemented \
coefficients; a unique nonlinear microfoundation is not asserted where it is \
not specified in the local files. Algebraic checks here do not replace \
solving or estimating the stochastic model. No new Dynare simulation or \
determinacy test is claimed.", "Text"], 
  Cell["1. Model map, notation and timing", "Section"], 
  Cell["Classic: one domestic consumption good, capital and labour in \
production, domestic inflation relevant for consumption and policy, and y \
used as GDP. It has 42 numbered core equations and eight innovations. \
Energy-food: a three-good consumption basket, energy as an imported \
intermediate input, imported final food, distinct CPI and domestic inflation, \
explicit GDP, 64 core equations and ten innovations. Energy-only retains both \
energy channels but removes food, leaving 60 core equations and nine \
innovations. Measurement equations are additional to these counts.", "Text"], 
  Cell["1.1 What an equilibrium model assumes", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["A DSGE model describes households and firms making decisions under \
constraints, and prices making those decisions mutually compatible. Dynamic \
means that choices today affect tomorrow: saving changes future resources, \
investment changes future capital, and a wage or price set today may remain \
effective for several periods. Stochastic means that agents face innovations \
they cannot perfectly forecast. General equilibrium means that consumption, \
production, wages, interest rates and policy are solved together, rather than \
changed one at a time while everything else is held fixed.", "Text"], 
  Cell["These words matter for reading a derivation. A firm's condition for a \
given output target is not yet an equilibrium output equation. A household's \
demand for energy at a given expenditure level is not yet the full \
consumption response to an energy shock. We first derive such conditional \
decisions, then combine them with resource constraints, price adjustment, \
policy and expectations. An impulse response follows from that complete \
system.", "Text"], Cell["1.2 Steady states, trends and local approximations", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["The steady state is the reference allocation around \
which deviations are measured. It need not imply that observed GDP is \
literally constant: after removing a balanced-growth trend, ratios and \
suitably scaled quantities can be constant while their original levels grow. \
Detrending is the operation that separates this predictable growth component \
from cyclical movements. Here tau, the gross trend-growth factor, enters \
capital accumulation and habit-adjusted consumption because yesterday's and \
today's levels are measured on different trend scales.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Log-linearisation approximates the equilibrium near that reference \
allocation. For a positive variable, its log change is approximately its \
percentage change for small movements. Thus a value of 2 in a 100-log \
deviation means a level about 2.02% above the reference, not two extra \
physical units. A linear equation is useful for tracing propagation and \
expectations, but it need not describe a large crisis or an allocation near a \
feasibility boundary. This is why positivity and steady-state restrictions \
are part of the mathematics, rather than optional technical footnotes.", 
   "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox[
         StyleBox["z", Italic, StripOnInput -> False], "^"], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{"100", "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{"log", 
          RowBox[{"(", FractionBox[RowBox[{FormBox[RowBox[{"Z", "(", "t", 
                  ")"}], TraditionalForm]}], RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[
                 "Z", "_"], TraditionalForm]}]], ")"}]}]}]}]], 
   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(1)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["Upper-case symbols denote positive levels. Lower-case quantities and \
relative prices denote 100 times log deviations from steady state, unless \
explicitly identified as rate, unemployment or shock variables with a \
different convention. Inflation and interest rates are quarterly \
percentage-point deviations. In the economic interpretation, forward-looking \
endogenous quantities are conditional expectations E_t; the code's (+1) is a \
lead, not a known future outcome. A lag is t\[Minus]1.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Do not apply the 100-log convention blindly to all shocks: epsilonp \
and epsilonw are explicitly multiplied by 100 in their equations. epsilong is \
already the additive government contribution in the resource equation. \
Unemployment u and un use the source's unemployment-rate convention. ryear=4r \
approximately annualises the quarterly rate.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
         StyleBox["E", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{"100", "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{"log", 
          RowBox[{"(", FractionBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[
                 {SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "E", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                    TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}], 
                RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "D", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                    TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]]}], 
             RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[SubscriptBox[
                     StyleBox["P", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> 
                       False], StyleBox["E", FontSlant -> Plain, 
                      StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], TraditionalForm]}], 
                RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "D", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], 
                   TraditionalForm]}]]}]], ")"}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(2)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
         StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{"100", "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{"log", 
          RowBox[{"(", FractionBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[
                 {SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "C", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                    TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}], 
                RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "D", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                    TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]]}], 
             RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[SubscriptBox[
                     StyleBox["P", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> 
                       False], StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> Italic, 
                      StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], TraditionalForm]}], 
                RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[SubscriptBox[StyleBox["P", 
                      FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False], StyleBox[
                      "D", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], 
                   TraditionalForm]}]]}]], ")"}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(3)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["pF is the analogous relative food price. pi is domestic-goods \
inflation, piC consumption inflation, and piE/piF imported-good inflation. w \
is the nominal wage deflated by PD; wc uses PC. rk is the rental return on \
capital services, distinct from the nominal bond rate r. k denotes services; \
kbar installed capital. X denotes technological output before fixed costs, \
Y=X\[Minus]\[CapitalPhi] output after the fixed cost, and GDP domestic value \
added after subtracting imported intermediate energy.", "Text"], 
  Cell["2. The classic benchmark and common dynamic core", "Section"], 
  Cell["2.1 Derive the consumption equation", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["A compatible reconstruction of the implemented coefficients uses log \
utility of an external-habit surplus S_t=C_t\[Minus]h C_(t\[Minus]1)^agg. The \
household treats the aggregate lagged habit as given; internal habit would \
add future-utility effects to the derivative. Detrending at gross growth \
\[Tau] gives an effective habit coefficient h*=h/\[Tau], with \
0\[LessEqual]h*<1. The surplus must remain positive.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Habit formation: consumption relative to a reference standard", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["Habit formation means that the satisfaction from \
current consumption depends on a reference level inherited from the past. \
Think of a household accustomed to a particular standard of living. A \
consumption level that previously seemed generous may feel restrictive after \
a period of higher expenditure. In the specification used to reconstruct this \
block, utility depends on surplus consumption S: actual consumption minus a \
fraction h of a lagged reference. Habit is therefore a change in preferences \
over consumption paths, rather than a literal extra purchase in the budget.", 
   "Text"], Cell["The parameter h measures the strength of the reference \
standard. If h=0, utility depends on current consumption alone. If h is \
positive, keeping consumption unchanged after a rise in the reference leaves \
a smaller surplus and therefore a higher marginal utility of resources. For a \
numerical illustration, with current consumption 100, lagged reference 90 and \
h=0.6, the surplus is 46. At the same current consumption but a reference of \
100, the surplus is 40. Under log-surplus utility, marginal utility rises \
from 1/46 to 1/40. These numbers illustrate preferences; they do not imply \
that expenditure is 46 or that 54 units disappear from production.", "Text"], 
  Cell["External and internal habit are different assumptions. With external \
habit, a small household compares itself with past aggregate consumption and \
treats that benchmark as outside its individual control. In a symmetric \
equilibrium, individual and aggregate consumption may have the same realised \
value, but they must not be treated as the same choice variable when \
differentiating. With internal habit, today's own consumption also changes \
tomorrow's own benchmark. Its lifetime derivative therefore includes an extra \
future-utility term. Substituting internal habit into a derivation based on \
external habit would generally change the Euler equation.", "Text"], 
  Cell["For the compatible external-habit reconstruction, the period utility \
contribution is log(S) less a labour-disutility term. Log utility is defined \
only for S>0. This says that consumption must exceed the habit-adjusted \
reference, a stronger requirement than merely C>0. We do not introduce a new \
freely estimated CRRA coefficient here: the unit curvature of log-surplus \
utility is what reproduces the simple c1 and c2 coefficients in the local \
files.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["uh", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{"log", RowBox[{"(", SubscriptBox[FormBox["S", 
              TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], ")"}]}], 
        RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{RowBox[{"\[ScriptV]", RowBox[{"(", SubscriptBox[
                 FormBox[StyleBox["N", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> 
                    False], TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], 
                ")"}]}]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(4)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["S", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> Italic, 
           StripOnInput -> False], TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
          TraditionalForm]], RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
           {FormBox["h", TraditionalForm], "\[ThinSpace]", 
            SubscriptBox[FormBox[TemplateBox[{StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> 
                  Italic, StripOnInput -> False], "agg"}, "Superscript", 
               SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"-", 
                 "1"}]}]]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(5)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["\[Lambda]", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"1"}], 
       RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["S", TraditionalForm], 
          FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(6)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["The nominal bond FOC is \[Lambda]_t=\[Beta] \
E_t[\[Lambda]_(t+1)R_t/\[CapitalPi]C_(t+1)], augmented by the model's \
risk-premium wedge. Marginal utility linearises as \
\[Lambda]-hat_t=\[Minus](c_t\[Minus]h*c_(t\[Minus]1))/(1\[Minus]h*). Insert \
this relation into the bond Euler equation, multiply by 1\[Minus]h*, collect \
the two terms containing c_t, and divide by 1+h*. The resulting consumption \
equation is:", "Text"], Cell["From marginal utility to an Euler equation", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["An Euler equation compares two feasible uses of one \
additional unit of resources: consume it today, or save it and consume its \
payoff tomorrow. The multiplier lambda measures the utility value of an extra \
unit of current purchasing power. The discount factor beta weights future \
utility. The gross nominal payoff must be divided by future consumption-price \
inflation because tomorrow's money buys goods at tomorrow's prices. Under \
uncertainty, expected discounted marginal utility times the real payoff is \
the relevant object; the expectation is conditional on information available \
today.", "Text"], Cell["The following two first-order relations make the \
intermediate algebra visible. The hat on lambda denotes its 100-log \
deviation. The interest-rate and premium terms follow the source's linear \
units. The first relation comes from differentiating log marginal utility \
around the positive trend-adjusted surplus; the second is the log-linear \
nominal bond condition. Together they explain why lagged consumption enters \
the final demand equation.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "^"], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      FractionBox[RowBox[
        {RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["c", 
                   TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], 
                 RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{FormBox[TemplateBox[{"h", "*"}, 
                       "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], 
                      TraditionalForm], "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[
                      FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", 
                         TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}]]}]}]}], 
               ")"}]}]}]}], RowBox[
        {RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{FormBox[TemplateBox[
                   {"h", "*"}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], 
                  TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}]}]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(7)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "^"], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", 
           FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[
           FormBox[OverscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "^"], TraditionalForm], 
           RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]], 
          "]"}], RowBox[{"+", SubscriptBox[FormBox["r", TraditionalForm], 
           FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}], 
        RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", 
               FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[FormBox[
                TemplateBox[{"\[Pi]", StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> Italic, 
                   StripOnInput -> False]}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> 
                  SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], RowBox[
                {FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]], 
              "]"}]}]}], RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
           {SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Epsilon]", "b"], 
              TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]}]], 
   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(8)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[
     {RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"+", FormBox[TemplateBox[
                {"h", "*"}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], 
               TraditionalForm]}]}], ")"}], "\[ThinSpace]", 
        SubscriptBox[FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
          TraditionalForm]]}], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", 
           FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[
           FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], RowBox[
            {FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]], "]"}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{FormBox[TemplateBox[{"h", "*"}, "Superscript", 
              SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
            "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"-", 
                 "1"}]}]]}]}], RowBox[
         {"-", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
                   {FormBox[TemplateBox[{"h", "*"}, "Superscript", 
                      SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm]}]}]}], 
              ")"}], "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SubscriptBox[
                 FormBox["r", TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
                  TraditionalForm]], RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{RowBox[
                     {SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", FormBox["t", 
                        TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[FormBox[
                        TemplateBox[{"\[Pi]", StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> 
                          Italic, StripOnInput -> False]}, "Superscript", 
                         SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
                       RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", 
                          "1"}]}]], "]"}]}]}], RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
                   {SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Epsilon]", "b"], 
                      TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}], 
              ")"}]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(9)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["Dividing the last line by 1+h* gives the implemented equation below. \
Habit adds a backward-looking element through past consumption while \
preserving a forward-looking element through expected consumption. It also \
changes the direct coefficient on the expected real rate. With h*=0, c1=c2=1 \
and there is no lagged-consumption term. As h* approaches one from below, c1 \
approaches one half, the lag weight approaches one half, and c2 approaches \
zero. This is a statement about coefficients in the conditional linear \
equation, not a guarantee that every general-equilibrium consumption response \
becomes smaller.", "Text"], Cell["Habit persistence must also be \
distinguished from shock persistence. Habit makes past consumption relevant \
even if an innovation itself is short lived. A persistent shock keeps a \
disturbance alive through its own stochastic law. A model can have both \
mechanisms; observing a slow consumption response alone does not identify \
which one generated it. The parameter restriction h*=h/tau<1 also protects \
the positive steady surplus after accounting for trend growth.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["c", "1"], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", 
             FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[
             FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", 
                TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]], "]"}]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", 
                  RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["c", "1"], 
                     TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}], "\[ThinSpace]", 
            SubscriptBox[FormBox["c", TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"-", 
                 "1"}]}]]}]}], RowBox[
         {"-", RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["c", "2"], TraditionalForm], 
            "\[ThinSpace]", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["r", 
                  TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], 
                RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{RowBox[{SubscriptBox[
                       "\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", FormBox["t", 
                        TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[FormBox[
                        TemplateBox[{"\[Pi]", StyleBox["C", FontSlant -> 
                          Italic, StripOnInput -> False]}, "Superscript", 
                         SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
                       RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", 
                          "1"}]}]], "]"}]}]}], RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
                   {SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Epsilon]", "b"], 
                      TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}], 
              ")"}]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(10)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["c", "1"], TraditionalForm], "=", 
      FractionBox[RowBox[{"1"}], RowBox[
        {RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"+", FormBox[TemplateBox[
                 {"h", "*"}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], 
                TraditionalForm]}]}], ")"}]}]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(11)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["c", "2"], TraditionalForm], "=", 
      FractionBox[RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
                {FormBox[TemplateBox[{"h", "*"}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> 
                    SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}]}], 
       RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"+", FormBox[TemplateBox[
                 {"h", "*"}, "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], 
                TraditionalForm]}]}], ")"}]}]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(12)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["In the implementation hstar=ch/ctau. Classic PC=PD implies piC=pi and \
recovers classic equation 01. Both sectoral versions use piC in equation 07. \
A CRRA curvature other than one would change the simple real-rate loading; \
the elementary CRRA-labour notebook is not a literal complete derivation of \
these habit coefficients. The precise structural origin of the \
financial/preference wedge epsilonb is not identified by its reduced-form \
loading alone.", "Text"], Cell["2.2 Capital, investment and utilisation", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["The inherited investment block corresponds to capital \
accumulation with investment efficiency and adjustment costs. The local \
linear files do not uniquely specify the nonlinear adjustment function. Its \
detrended first-order laws are therefore stated with the actual coefficients, \
rather than attributed to a unique unobserved level function.", "Text"], 
  Cell["What capital adjustment costs and Tobin's q mean", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["Investment is a flow of newly created capital; installed capital is a \
stock accumulated over time. Depreciation delta is the fraction of the \
existing stock lost in a period. If delta=0.025, 2.5% depreciates per quarter \
under the stated quarterly timing. Capital services are the productive flow \
delivered by that stock, which can also vary with how intensively machinery \
is used. These distinctions explain why the model needs i, kbar, k and v \
rather than one undifferentiated capital variable.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Adjustment costs express the idea that rapidly changing the \
investment programme is costly. Hiring installation teams, reorganising \
production or absorbing new equipment cannot always be done instantly. The \
cost is associated with changing investment, so the optimality condition \
involves past and expected future investment as well as q. This provides \
economic inertia without assuming that firms simply ignore profitable \
opportunities. The investment-efficiency process is a separate disturbance to \
how effective investment is; its precise nonlinear placement cannot be \
recovered uniquely from the linear equations alone.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Tobin's q is the shadow value of an additional unit of installed \
capital relative to the price of acquiring investment goods. Around the \
chosen reference, a positive q deviation makes investment more attractive. \
The investment equation translates that incentive into an adjustment path. A \
capital-value equation then links today's q to tomorrow's rental payoff and \
resale value, discounted by the relevant return. This makes q a \
forward-looking valuation variable, not a new physical quantity produced by \
firms.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox[
         StyleBox["k", Italic, StripOnInput -> False], "_"], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["k", "1"], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox[
             StyleBox["k", Italic, StripOnInput -> False], "_"], 
            TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}]]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", 
                  RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["k", "1"], 
                     TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}], "\[ThinSpace]", 
            SubscriptBox[FormBox["i", TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
              TraditionalForm]]}]}], RowBox[
         {"+", RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm], 
            "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Epsilon]", 
               "q"], TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
              TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(13)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["i", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["i", "1"], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox["i", TraditionalForm], 
           RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}]]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", 
                  RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["i", "1"], 
                     TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}], "\[ThinSpace]", 
            RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalE]", FormBox["t", 
                TraditionalForm]], "[", SubscriptBox[FormBox["i", 
                TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], 
                 RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]], "]"}]}]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["i", "2"], 
             TraditionalForm], "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[
             FormBox["q", TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", 
              TraditionalForm]]}]}], RowBox[{"+", SubscriptBox[
           FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Epsilon]", "q"], TraditionalForm], 
           FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(14)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["k", "1"], TraditionalForm], "=", 
      FractionBox[RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[
                {FormBox["\[Delta]", TraditionalForm]}]}]}], ")"}]}], 
       RowBox[{FormBox["\[Tau]", TraditionalForm]}]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(15)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["i", "1"], TraditionalForm], "=", 
