   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(91)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["s", "CN"], 
         TraditionalForm], RowBox[{"+", FractionBox["I", "Y"]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", FormBox[SubscriptBox["s", "G"], TraditionalForm]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", FormBox[SubscriptBox["s", "X"], TraditionalForm]}]}], 
      "=", "1"}]], "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> 
    {{None, "(92)"}, {None, None}}], Cell["In food calibration, cshareCF=sCF, \
cshareME=sME, cweightXE=\[Theta]XE and cweightXF=\[Theta]XF. Energy-only has \
\[Omega]F=0 and sME=sX; its cweightEn and cweightCe may therefore be divided \
by cshareX equivalently. The generators handle zero denominators, whose \
economic interpretation is a reduced no-import component.", "Text"], 
  Cell["7.1 The wage and the classic-model limit", "Subsection"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox["w", "_"], TraditionalForm], 
      "=", SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", FractionBox[
          RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox[FormBox["\[Alpha]", 
                TraditionalForm], FormBox["\[Alpha]", TraditionalForm]], 
              "\[ThinSpace]", SuperscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", 
                 StyleBox["N", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                TraditionalForm], FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", 
                 StyleBox["N", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
                TraditionalForm]], "\[ThinSpace]", SuperscriptBox[FormBox[
                "\[Nu]", TraditionalForm], FormBox["\[Nu]", 
                TraditionalForm]]}]}], RowBox[
           {RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Psi]", StyleBox["P", FontSlant -> 
                  Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], TraditionalForm], 
              "\[ThinSpace]", SuperscriptBox[FormBox[OverscriptBox[
                 SubscriptBox["R", StyleBox["K", FontSlant -> Italic, 
                   StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], TraditionalForm], FormBox[
                "\[Alpha]", TraditionalForm]]}]}]], ")"}], 
       FractionBox[RowBox[{"1"}], RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", 
            StyleBox["N", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
           TraditionalForm]}]]]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(93)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{FractionBox["N", "K"], "=", 
      RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", 
             StyleBox["N", FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], 
            TraditionalForm]}], RowBox[{FormBox["\[Alpha]", 
            TraditionalForm]}]], "\[ThinSpace]", FractionBox[
         RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox[SubscriptBox["R", StyleBox["K", 
               FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], "_"], 
            TraditionalForm]}], RowBox[{FormBox[OverscriptBox["w", "_"], 
            TraditionalForm]}]]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(94)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["This wage follows by imposing m\:0304=1/\[Psi]P in the dual cost \
function with unit relative energy price. At \[Nu]=0 use the reduced formula \
without \[Nu]^\[Nu], interpreted by its continuous limit. The classic \
generator writes K/Y=\[Psi]P(N/K)^(\[Alpha]\[Minus]1); the energy generator \
writes K/Y=\[Alpha]/R\:0304K. With the consistent classic wage these are \
algebraically equal, as verified symbolically. Their different appearances do \
not by themselves reveal an economic discrepancy.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Require ccy>0, R\:0304K>0, \[Alpha]N>0, sGDP>0 and positive steady \
output. If these fail, the intended steady allocation is not admissible. \
Trend, units and markup/fixed-cost normalisations must remain matched in any \
nesting comparison.", "Text"], 
  Cell["7.2 What a calibrated share does and does not identify", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["A calibrated share fixes a reference allocation or \
parameter value; it is not automatically an estimate of a behavioural \
response. omegaE is a household expenditure weight, while nu is a production \
elasticity and a variable-cost share. Identifying nu with an \
energy-expenditure-to-output ratio uses the additional steady markup and \
fixed-cost normalisations derived above. Identifying it with an energy-to-GDP \
ratio would require another denominator conversion. The numerical value of \
the same word share therefore depends on exactly what is in the numerator and \
denominator.", "Text"], Cell["Similarly, a policy coefficient, a shock \
persistence and a substitution elasticity describe different mechanisms. A \
policy coefficient determines how the central bank reacts; a persistence \
coefficient determines how a disturbance evolves; an elasticity describes a \
conditional response inside an optimisation problem. Comparing them only as \
large or small numbers would obscure both units and economic meaning.", 
   "Text"], Cell["8. Flexible prices, shocks and transmission channels", 
   "Section"], Cell["8.1 Derive the flexible counterfactual consistently", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["Suffix f denotes the flexible-price counterfactual. \
Both sectoral economies face the same exogenous relative commodity prices. \
The flexible equations remove the sticky allocation's time-varying markup \
wedges, while retaining the steady markup normalisation. mc_f=0 means ZERO \
DEVIATION, not a zero level of marginal cost.", "Text"], 
  Cell["The flexible-price allocation asks what the same economy would do if \
its nominal adjustment restrictions were removed under the maintained \
counterfactual rules. It does not remove technology, resource constraints or \
imported-price disturbances. Consequently, a costly energy shock can reduce \
the flexible allocation itself. Actual output can fall while the output gap \
moves little if natural output falls by a similar amount. Potential or \
natural output here is therefore model dependent rather than an externally \
observed smooth trend.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"0", "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", StyleBox["N", 
             FontSlant -> Italic, StripOnInput -> False]], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox[TemplateBox[{"w", "f"}, 
             "Superscript", SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
           FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", RowBox[{FormBox["\[Alpha]", TraditionalForm], 
