                    SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
                  FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}], ")"}]}], 
       RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["s", "GDP"], TraditionalForm]}]]}]], 
   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(102)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["gap", TraditionalForm], 
       FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox["gdp", TraditionalForm], 
         FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], 
        RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[TemplateBox[{StyleBox[
                 "gdp", Italic, StripOnInput -> False], "f"}, "Superscript", 
               SyntaxForm -> SuperscriptBox], TraditionalForm], 
             FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]}]], "DisplayFormula", 
   CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(103)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["The flexible resource and export-financing equations use flexible \
quantities, as in equations 34-36; using sticky quantities would contaminate \
natural output. Commodity prices can change natural GDP and actual GDP \
together, so the GDP response and the output-gap response need not have the \
same sign or magnitude. The flexible capital, investment, utilisation, \
employment and trend-consumption equations preserve the corresponding common \
block.", "Text"], Cell["8.2 Commodity relative-price processes", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["A shock innovation is the new, unpredictable \
component arriving this period. A shock process includes both that innovation \
and the inherited effect of earlier innovations. In pE_t=rhoE \
pE_(t\[Minus]1)+etaE_t, rhoE measures persistence and the standard deviation \
of etaE measures the typical innovation scale. A large one-period innovation \
can occur in a short-lived process, and a small innovation can have a long \
effect in a highly persistent process. These parameters should not be \
interpreted interchangeably.", "Text"], Cell["For a positive persistence \
below one, the conditional expected relative price gradually returns toward \
its reference. Its approximate half-life is Log[1/2]/Log[rhoE] periods, when \
0<rhoE<1. The restriction is needed: the formula is not meaningful at rhoE=0 \
or one, and negative persistence implies alternating signs. The process is a \
law for a relative price, so it cannot by itself identify a structural \
oil-supply shock, food harvest failure or foreign-demand shock.", "Text"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
         StyleBox["E", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", StyleBox["E", 
             FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
             StyleBox["E", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
            TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}]]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Eta]", 
             StyleBox["E", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
            TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]], 
   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(104)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
         StyleBox["F", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
        TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]], "=", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", StyleBox["F", 
             FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], TraditionalForm], 
          "\[ThinSpace]", SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["p", 
             StyleBox["F", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
            TraditionalForm], RowBox[{FormBox["t", TraditionalForm], 
             RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}]]}], 
        RowBox[{"+", SubscriptBox[FormBox[SubscriptBox["\[Eta]", 
             StyleBox["F", FontSlant -> Plain, StripOnInput -> False]], 
            TraditionalForm], FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]]}]}]}]], 
   "DisplayFormula", CellFrameLabels -> {{None, "(105)"}, {None, None}}], 
  Cell["These are reduced-form relative PRICE LEVELS in domestic currency, \
not commodity inflation rates. |\[Rho]|<1 gives a stationary AR(1). The food \
model adds etaF and its innovation scale. Neither specification identifies \
whether a commodity-price innovation reflects global supply, global demand or \
exchange rates; there is no explicit foreign energy or agriculture supply \
sector or separate retail pass-through dynamics.", "Text"], 
  Cell["An isolated innovation \[Eta] gives p_j=\[Rho]^j\[Eta] for \
j\[GreaterEqual]0. Its direct CPI-inflation effect is \[Omega]\[Eta] on \
impact and \[Omega](\[Rho]\[Minus]1)\[Rho]^(j\[Minus]1)\[Eta] thereafter. For \
0<\[Rho]<1, the later direct contribution is negative as the relative price \
returns to baseline. Domestic inflation and equilibrium feedback add to that \
component. A permanent price-level innovation at \[Rho]=1 is not permanently \
higher inflation; it also changes the stationary-state interpretation.", 
   "Text"], Cell["8.3 Inherited disturbances and sign conventions", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["The common block contains AR(1) risk-premium, \
investment-efficiency, productivity, labour-supply and monetary-policy \
processes. Government spending has an AR(1) plus crhoga times the \
contemporaneous technology innovation. Price and wage markup processes are \
ARMA(1,1): \
epsilonp=crhop\[CenterDot]epsilonp(\[Minus]1)+etap\[Minus]cmup\[CenterDot]eta\
p(\[Minus]1), with the analogous wage equation. Preserve both innovation \
scales and the explicit factors of 100. A positive epsilonr lowers the policy \
rate directly; a positive epsilonp enters domestic inflation with the \
negative sign written in Section 4.", "Text"], 
  Cell["9. The requested sequence: nesting versus recalibration", "Section"], 
  Cell["Classic to energy-food adds the consumption price basket, energy \
input in production, import financing, GDP double deflation, CPI-based wages \
and policy, two commodity-price processes, and the flexible counterparts. It \
is not a food-only change. Food to energy-only sets \[Omega]F=0, removes food \
quantities, prices, measurement variables and etaF, drops equations 61-64, \
removes the food term from equation 01, and reduces export-financing \
equations 11 and 35 to the energy-only identity. Recompute all affected \
shares. Technology, marginal cost and the energy-input FOC remain \
structurally the same.", "Text"], Cell["At matched common parameters, a zero \
food share makes a food shock irrelevant to aggregate allocations. Recovering \
the classic model additionally requires \[Nu]=\[Omega]E=0, removal of \
inactive commodity variables, consistent limiting shares and units. Then \
pc=0, wc=w, piC=pi, GDP=Y and import-financing exports disappear. This \
nesting statement is about the equations. It does not imply equality of \
current impulse responses across folders with different fitted parameters.", 
   "Text"], Cell["9.1 EA20 calibration snapshot", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["The following entries are read from the current EA20 calibration \
modules. They identify changes in parameter values separately from structural \
modifications. Food is a simulation prototype from a diagnostic energy \
variant, not a fresh estimation of the whole food extension.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Parameter                   Classic     Energy-food    \
Energy-only\nch: external habit          0.635318    0.461469       \
0.412019\ncthetap: price rigidity      0.842802    0.750000       \
0.990000\ncthetaw: wage rigidity       0.696311    0.521709       \
0.502120\ncrpi: inflation feedback     1.287264    1.500000       \
0.438463\ncrhointr: rate smoothing     0.856383    0.967056       \
0.957115\ncrhoE: energy persistence    absent      0.966082       \
0.947039\ncsigmaE: energy innovation   absent      3.150063       \
3.171694\ncrhoF: food persistence      absent      0.800000       \
absent\ncsigmaF: food innovation     absent      5.000000       absent", 
   "Program", FontSize -> 9, CellHorizontalScrolling -> False], 
  Cell["The food README specifies illustrative food parameters, price \
rigidity 0.75 and inflation feedback 1.5; its main file simulates 200-quarter \
responses and does not execute estimation. The current energy-only file \
supports maximum likelihood when RUN_ESTIMATION=1; its default macro is zero \
and simulates the energy innovation. The classic file performs a stochastic \
simulation. These execution settings are not theoretical mechanisms.", 
   "Text"], Cell["The energy-only rigidity close to 0.99 and inflation \
feedback below one are reported, not endorsed or altered here. Determinacy \
cannot be inferred from that feedback coefficient alone: it requires the full \
system's eigenvalue and rank checks at the chosen parameter vector. A \
controlled comparison should match common parameters, shock units, timing and \
size, and the sample/observables when estimating. Report structural and \
recalibration effects separately.", "Text"], 
  Cell["10. Measurement equations and empirical interpretation", "Section"], 
  Cell["Classic measured GDP growth uses y\[Minus]y(\[Minus]1); sectoral \
versions use gdp\[Minus]gdp(\[Minus]1), adding the same ctaubar+cebar trend. \
Consumption and investment growth retain their own log differences. Classic \
piobs=cpibar+pi becomes sectoral piobs=cpibar+piC. The observables pEobs=pE \
and pFobs=pF are demeaned relative price LEVELS, not inflation series.", 
   "Text"], Cell["Classic:      dyobs = ctaubar + cebar + y - \
y(-1);\nSectoral:     dyobs = ctaubar + cebar + gdp - gdp(-1);\nClassic:      \
piobs = cpibar + pi;\nSectoral:     piobs = cpibar + piC;\nAll models:   \
robs/4 = crbar + r;", "Program", FontSize -> 9, CellHorizontalScrolling -> 
    False], Cell["An additional measurement change is independent of \
commodity theory. Classic uses \
dwobs\[Minus]piobs=ctaubar+w\[Minus]w(\[Minus]1)\[Minus](pi\[Minus]pi(\
\[Minus]1)). Sectoral versions use \
dwobs=ctaubar+w\[Minus]w(\[Minus]1)+cpibar+pi. The latter is compatible with \
nominal wage growth when w is the domestic-real wage. Substituting classic \
piobs into its wage relation gives a different inflation timing. Reconcile \
this with the actual data before attributing likelihood differences to energy \
or food.", "Text"], Cell["The food EA20 measurement file states that no local \
observations are available, and the main file does not estimate. Declaring \
pFobs is an interface, not proof of a suitable dataset. A global agricultural \
index, imported food price and retail HICP food index need not measure the \
same object. Additional pass-through mechanisms may be needed if the selected \
data differ from the model's import-price concept.", "Text"], 
  Cell["ryear=4r, while piyear sums four quarterly CPI-inflation deviations \
in the sectoral versions; piDyear sums domestic inflation. Classic sums \
domestic inflation because CPI equals it. Restore trend intercepts before \
comparing model deviations with observed rate levels. Country-specific \
modules may differ from the EA20 snapshot documented here.", "Text"], 
  Cell["11. Boundary conditions, interpretation and exercises", "Section"], 
  Cell["Positive-domain calculations. Production requires positive \
productivity, prices, inputs and interior shares. Its continuous extension is \
zero if any essential input is zero. Holding other inputs positive and fixed, \
each own marginal product tends to infinity at its zero boundary and to zero \
at infinity. Simultaneous limits can depend on the path. Do not apply \
logarithms or divide by a zero input directly at the boundary.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Reduced models and feasibility. Require \[Alpha]+\[Nu]<1 and \
\[Omega]E+\[Omega]F<1 in the interior model, positive habit surplus, \
0<\[Beta]<1, admissible depreciation and trend, and ccy>0. At a zero input or \
consumption share use the reduced technology/basket, not an expression \
containing 0/0 or 0^0. For fixed \[CapitalPhi], Y\:0304 must be positive; as \
Y\:0304 approaches zero, \[Psi]P grows and the local log approximation \
becomes poorly behaved.", "Text"], Cell["Intertemporal boundaries. Initial \
installed capital is predetermined. Infinite-horizon optimisation needs a \
debt no-Ponzi admissibility condition and an appropriate transversality \
condition under the selected asset and multiplier conventions. These economic \
restrictions differ from Dynare's local Blanchard-Kahn conditions. Neither \
follows from a static FOC or from the algebraic checks below.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Transmission. Energy affects the consumption deflator and directly \
enters the firm's unit cost. Food here affects final consumption, purchasing \
power, policy and export financing, but does not directly enter the \
production cost function. The trade closure and substitution toward domestic \
goods can produce an initially positive GDP response; the identities do not \
impose its sign. The whole dynamic system must be solved. First-order local \
models do not automatically capture large-shock corners or nonlinear welfare \
effects.", "Text"], Cell["Questions and short answers", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["1. Why en=mc+y/cpsip\[Minus]pE? The FOC contains X and x=y/cpsip. 2. \
Why no pE in real GDP? Intermediate energy is subtracted at fixed base \
prices. 3. Is wc just a new label for the wage equation? Nominal wage growth \
is a unit identity, but CPI indexation is a policy of wage adjustment. 4. \
Does the final folder add production energy to the food model? No; both \
already have production energy, and the final folder removes food. 5. Does \
\[Omega]F=0 alone replicate current energy-only IRFs? Only after matching all \
common parameters and shock definitions. 6. Does flexible mc=0 mean zero \
costs? No, it means zero marginal-cost deviation around the steady markup.", 
   "Text"], Cell["12. Equation map, sources and verification", "Section"], 
  Cell["Block                         Classic labels     Sectoral labels\nCPI \
and consumption basket     01                 01-07; food 61-64\nInvestment / \
capital value     02-03              08-09\nImports and resources          04 \
                10-12\nTechnology and factor choice   05,07,16           \
13-16\nDomestic price Phillips curve  06                 \
17\nWages/unemployment/trend       08-12              18-22\nCapital and \
utilisation        13-15              23-25\nPolicy and employment          \
17-18              26-27\nValue added                    y used as GDP      \
28\nFlexible counterfactual        19-31              29-47; food 62\nOutput \
gap                     32                 48\nEnergy-price process           \
absent             49\nInherited shocks               33-40              \
50-57\nAnnual indicators              41-42              58-60\nFood price \
and inflation       absent             food 63-64", "Program", FontSize -> 9, 
   CellHorizontalScrolling -> False], Cell["Numbered labels refer to the main \
.mod files; measurement includes add equations separately. The source hashes \
below identify the exact main-file snapshot used. These model files were read \
